msgid "**Role Groups:** Every LDAP sign-in refreshes name and email from the directory and sets the account flags from the role groups: ``administrator`` grants superuser and staff, ``moderator``, ``librarian`` and ``settings-manager`` grant the moderation, library and settings permission. A user outside a group loses its flag at the next sign-in. The settings module `templates/settings.py.j2 <./templates/settings.py.j2>`__ extends ``config.settings.production``."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Groups:** Every LDAP sign-in refreshes name and email from the directory and sets the account flags from the role groups: `administrator` grants superuser and staff, `moderator`, `librarian` and `settings-manager` grant the moderation, library and settings permission. A user outside a group loses its flag at the next sign-in. The settings module [templates/settings.py.j2](./templates/settings.py.j2) extends `config.settings.production`."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Groups:** Every OIDC registration and login sets ``admin`` from the ``administrator`` role group and the ``user_manager`` role from the ``user-manager`` role group (`files/ruby/initializers/infinito_rbac.rb <./files/ruby/initializers/infinito_rbac.rb>`__). Only accounts with an OIDC identity change; the administrator the deploy seeds keeps its rights."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Groups:** Every OIDC registration and login sets `admin` from the `administrator` role group and the `user_manager` role from the `user-manager` role group ([files/ruby/initializers/infinito_rbac.rb](./files/ruby/initializers/infinito_rbac.rb)). Only accounts with an OIDC identity change; the administrator the deploy seeds keeps its rights."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Groups:** On every new session [files/sso/header_auth.py](./files/sso/header_auth.py) sets the BookWyrm groups `admin`, `owner`, `moderator` and `editor` from the `administrator`, `owner`, `moderator` and `editor` role groups and grants staff with `admin`. The platform administrator that [tasks/01_site.yml](./tasks/01_site.yml) converges keeps its groups."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Groups:** On every new session `files/sso/header_auth.py <./files/sso/header_auth.py>`__ sets the BookWyrm groups ``admin``, ``owner``, ``moderator`` and ``editor`` from the ``administrator``, ``owner``, ``moderator`` and ``editor`` role groups and grants staff with ``admin``. The platform administrator that `tasks/01_site.yml <./tasks/01_site.yml>`__ converges keeps its groups."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Groups:** [files/elixir/rbac.exs](./files/elixir/rbac.exs) creates the Keycloak accounts of the `administrator` and `moderator` role group members that have none yet and sets the role of every Keycloak account to administrator, moderator or user from its groups. The platform administrator keeps its role. [files/python/rbac_sync.py](./files/python/rbac_sync.py) runs it through `mobilizon rpc` as a host hook of [sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync](../sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync/) at every deploy and on its timer, so a group change in Keycloak or LDAP reaches Mobilizon within one timer interval."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Groups:** [files/sql/reconcile-roles.sql](./files/sql/reconcile-roles.sql) creates the accounts of the `administrator` and `collaborator` role group members that have none yet and sets the role of every account to administrator, collaborator or visitor from its groups. It runs through `psql` in the database container as an RBAC hook of [sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync](../sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync/) at every deploy and on its timer, so a group change reaches Fider within one timer interval. The platform administrator keeps its role."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Groups:** `files/elixir/rbac.exs <./files/elixir/rbac.exs>`__ creates the Keycloak accounts of the ``administrator`` and ``moderator`` role group members that have none yet and sets the role of every Keycloak account to administrator, moderator or user from its groups. The platform administrator keeps its role. `files/python/rbac_sync.py <./files/python/rbac_sync.py>`__ runs it through ``mobilizon rpc`` as a host hook of `sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync <../sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync/>`__ at every deploy and on its timer, so a group change in Keycloak or LDAP reaches Mobilizon within one timer interval."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Groups:** `files/sql/reconcile-roles.sql <./files/sql/reconcile-roles.sql>`__ creates the accounts of the ``administrator`` and ``collaborator`` role group members that have none yet and sets the role of every account to administrator, collaborator or visitor from its groups. It runs through ``psql`` in the database container as an RBAC hook of `sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync <../sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync/>`__ at every deploy and on its timer, so a group change reaches Fider within one timer interval. The platform administrator keeps its role."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Mapping At Sign-In:** The role-local plugin [files/php/infinito_rbac_login.php](./files/php/infinito_rbac_login.php) applies the same rules to the account that signs in through RebelOIDC, read from the `groups` claim of the ID token of that sign-in, so a role-group change and a website added later reach the account at its next sign-in instead of the next timer run. A sign-in without a `groups` claim changes nothing, a password login is never touched, and neither are the local superuser and the anonymous account. The deploy installs and activates the plugin together with RebelOIDC and writes its group paths to the `[InfinitoRbacLogin]` section of `config/config.ini.php`."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Mapping At Sign-In:** The role-local plugin `files/php/infinito_rbac_login.php <./files/php/infinito_rbac_login.php>`__ applies the same rules to the account that signs in through RebelOIDC, read from the ``groups`` claim of the ID token of that sign-in, so a role-group change and a website added later reach the account at its next sign-in instead of the next timer run. A sign-in without a ``groups`` claim changes nothing, a password login is never touched, and neither are the local superuser and the anonymous account. The deploy installs and activates the plugin together with RebelOIDC and writes its group paths to the ``[InfinitoRbacLogin]`` section of ``config/config.ini.php``."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Mapping:** Every realm user signs in as a regular Gitea user. Members of the ``administrator`` role group become Gitea administrators and members of the ``restricted`` role group become restricted users at each sign-in, through the LDAP admin and restricted filters and through the admin and restricted groups of the OIDC source, which reads the groups claim."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Mapping:** Every realm user signs in as a regular Gitea user. Members of the `administrator` role group become Gitea administrators and members of the `restricted` role group become restricted users at each sign-in, through the LDAP admin and restricted filters and through the admin and restricted groups of the OIDC source, which reads the groups claim."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Mapping:** Members of the ``manager`` and ``coursecreator`` RBAC groups hold Moodle’s Manager and Course creator roles in the system context, members of the ``administrator`` group are site administrators. ``auth_ldap`` maps the ``manager`` and ``coursecreator`` group DNs at every LDAP login. With ``sso`` or ``ldap`` enabled, ``tasks/07_rbac.yml`` registers ```files/php/provision/rbac.php`` <./files/php/provision/rbac.php>`__ as an RBAC hook of `sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync <../sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync/>`__, which converges both system roles and ``$CFG->siteadmins`` at every deploy and on its timer: it creates the account of a member that has none yet, assigns the roles of every member and removes them from every other account. The bootstrap administrator stays the first site administrator, and the bootstrap administrator, the MCP account and ``guest`` keep their role assignments."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Mapping:** Members of the `manager` and `coursecreator` RBAC groups hold Moodle's Manager and Course creator roles in the system context, members of the `administrator` group are site administrators. `auth_ldap` maps the `manager` and `coursecreator` group DNs at every LDAP login. With `sso` or `ldap` enabled, `tasks/07_rbac.yml` registers [`files/php/provision/rbac.php`](./files/php/provision/rbac.php) as an RBAC hook of [sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync](../sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync/), which converges both system roles and `$CFG->siteadmins` at every deploy and on its timer: it creates the account of a member that has none yet, assigns the roles of every member and removes them from every other account. The bootstrap administrator stays the first site administrator, and the bootstrap administrator, the MCP account and `guest` keep their role assignments."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Mapping:** The RBAC roles ``view``, ``write`` and ``admin`` grant that access on every tracked website, and ``administrator`` grants superuser access. `files/php/rbac_reconcile.php <./files/php/rbac_reconcile.php>`__ creates an account for each member, assigns the access and takes it from every other account; the local superuser and the anonymous account are never touched. It runs as an RBAC hook of `sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync <../sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync/>`__ at every deploy and, with OpenLDAP deployed, on its timer, so a membership change and a website added later take effect within one timer interval."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Mapping:** The RBAC roles `view`, `write` and `admin` grant that access on every tracked website, and `administrator` grants superuser access. [files/php/rbac_reconcile.php](./files/php/rbac_reconcile.php) creates an account for each member, assigns the access and takes it from every other account; the local superuser and the anonymous account are never touched. It runs as an RBAC hook of [sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync](../sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync/) at every deploy and, with OpenLDAP deployed, on its timer, so a membership change and a website added later take effect within one timer interval."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Mapping:** With SSO, every login sets the OpenCloud role from the Keycloak realm roles: members of the ``administrator``, ``spaceadmin`` and ``guest`` role groups become ``admin``, ``spaceadmin`` and ``user-light``, and every other realm user signs in as ``user`` through ``default-roles-<realm>``. Without SSO, every account signs in as ``user``."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Mapping:** With SSO, every login sets the OpenCloud role from the Keycloak realm roles: members of the `administrator`, `spaceadmin` and `guest` role groups become `admin`, `spaceadmin` and `user-light`, and every other realm user signs in as `user` through `default-roles-<realm>`. Without SSO, every account signs in as `user`."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Reconcile:** [files/ruby/reconcile_rbac.rb](./files/ruby/reconcile_rbac.rb) makes the members of the `administrator` role group GitLab administrators and the members of the `external` role group external users, and revokes both flags from every other human account except `root` and the platform administrator. It runs as a container hook of [sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync](../sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync/) at every deploy and on its timer, so a member who has not signed in yet gets the role on the first run after the first sign-in."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role Reconcile:** `files/ruby/reconcile_rbac.rb <./files/ruby/reconcile_rbac.rb>`__ makes the members of the ``administrator`` role group GitLab administrators and the members of the ``external`` role group external users, and revokes both flags from every other human account except ``root`` and the platform administrator. It runs as a container hook of `sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync <../sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync/>`__ at every deploy and on its timer, so a member who has not signed in yet gets the role on the first run after the first sign-in."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role catalogue:** [catalogue.py](../../utils/roles/rbac/catalogue.py) builds the roles of every deployed application from its `meta/rbac.yml` when the role deploys. The catalogue preselects the least-privileged role and marks administrator roles. A role is an administrator role when its name is `administrator` or ends in `-administrator`, or when it carries `realm_management` roles, such as `user-manager` of [web-app-keycloak](../web-app-keycloak/meta/rbac.yml)."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role catalogue:** `catalogue.py <../../utils/roles/rbac/catalogue.py>`__ builds the roles of every deployed application from its ``meta/rbac.yml`` when the role deploys. The catalogue preselects the least-privileged role and marks administrator roles. A role is an administrator role when its name is ``administrator`` or ends in ``-administrator``, or when it carries ``realm_management`` roles, such as ``user-manager`` of `web-app-keycloak <../web-app-keycloak/meta/rbac.yml>`__."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role group sync:** With SSO or LDAP, [files/sql/rbac/reconcile.sql](./files/sql/rbac/reconcile.sql) runs through `psql` in the database container as an RBAC hook of [sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync](../sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync/) at every deploy and on its timer, so a group change reaches Semaphore within one timer interval. The same run creates every member of the `administrator` and `operator` role groups that has an e-mail and no account yet as an external Semaphore account, admin only for `administrator`, so a member added between deploys has its account within one timer interval."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role group sync:** With SSO or LDAP, `files/sql/rbac/reconcile.sql <./files/sql/rbac/reconcile.sql>`__ runs through ``psql`` in the database container as an RBAC hook of `sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync <../sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync/>`__ at every deploy and on its timer, so a group change reaches Semaphore within one timer interval. The same run creates every member of the ``administrator`` and ``operator`` role groups that has an e-mail and no account yet as an external Semaphore account, admin only for ``administrator``, so a member added between deploys has its account within one timer interval."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role groups at Keycloak sign-in:** with SSO, the role-owned Frappe app [`infinito_rbac`](./files/python/infinito_rbac/infinito_rbac/hooks.py) applies the same grants to the account that signs in. [`files/Dockerfile`](./files/Dockerfile) builds it into the image every Frappe container runs, and [`files/python/apply/login/rbac.py`](./files/python/apply/login/rbac.py) installs it on the site and writes its settings (`ERPNEXT_LOGIN_RBAC_SETTINGS`) into the site config. Its `override_whitelisted_methods` hook replaces both Frappe callbacks that complete a Keycloak sign-in, `login_via_keycloak` and the generic `custom/keycloak`, with [`login.py`](./files/python/infinito_rbac/infinito_rbac/login.py). It first refuses a sign-in whose `email_verified` claim is not `true`, a sign-in onto an account whose `keycloak` or `directory` `User Social Login` row names another userid (the claim the Social Login Key's `user_id_property` names), a sign-in onto an unlinked account by a user outside every role group, and a sign-in with a changed address while an account still carries the former one; a refused sign-in changes no account. Only then does it read the `RBAC.GROUP.CLAIM` claim from the userinfo before Frappe decides the sign-in: a member without an account is created and signs in at once, a member gets the roles of its groups and loses the managed roles of the groups it left, first and last name follow the `given_name` and `family_name` claims, and an account outside every role group loses the managed roles and, unless it still holds a role the mapping does not manage, is disabled, so Frappe refuses it. The sign-in changes no account but the one that signs in. The protected users stay untouched, and a userinfo without the groups claim fails the sign-in without a change. The timer hook stays the authority for every account that does not sign in."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role groups at Keycloak sign-in:** with SSO, the role-owned Frappe app ```infinito_rbac`` <./files/python/infinito_rbac/infinito_rbac/hooks.py>`__ applies the same grants to the account that signs in. ```files/Dockerfile`` <./files/Dockerfile>`__ builds it into the image every Frappe container runs, and ```files/python/apply/login/rbac.py`` <./files/python/apply/login/rbac.py>`__ installs it on the site and writes its settings (``ERPNEXT_LOGIN_RBAC_SETTINGS``) into the site config. Its ``override_whitelisted_methods`` hook replaces both Frappe callbacks that complete a Keycloak sign-in, ``login_via_keycloak`` and the generic ``custom/keycloak``, with ```login.py`` <./files/python/infinito_rbac/infinito_rbac/login.py>`__. It first refuses a sign-in whose ``email_verified`` claim is not ``true``, a sign-in onto an account whose ``keycloak`` or ``directory`` ``User Social Login`` row names another userid (the claim the Social Login Key’s ``user_id_property`` names), a sign-in onto an unlinked account by a user outside every role group, and a sign-in with a changed address while an account still carries the former one; a refused sign-in changes no account. Only then does it read the ``RBAC.GROUP.CLAIM`` claim from the userinfo before Frappe decides the sign-in: a member without an account is created and signs in at once, a member gets the roles of its groups and loses the managed roles of the groups it left, first and last name follow the ``given_name`` and ``family_name`` claims, and an account outside every role group loses the managed roles and, unless it still holds a role the mapping does not manage, is disabled, so Frappe refuses it. The sign-in changes no account but the one that signs in. The protected users stay untouched, and a userinfo without the groups claim fails the sign-in without a change. The timer hook stays the authority for every account that does not sign in."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role groups at sign-in:** [`files/Dockerfile`](./files/Dockerfile) installs the role-owned Composer package [`files/php/login_rbac/`](./files/php/login_rbac/composer.json) from a path repository, and Laravel's package discovery loads its service provider, which registers [`SyncRolesOnLogin`](./files/php/login_rbac/src/sync_roles_on_login.php) for every `Illuminate\\Auth\\Events\\Login`. It applies the rules of the RBAC reconcile to the account that signs in: `superuser` for the `administrator` role group, the `web-app-snipe-it-user` permission group for both role groups, neither for anyone else. An SSO sign-in takes its role groups from the `X-Forwarded-Groups` header of oauth2-proxy, which Apache copies into `INFINITO_SSO_GROUPS` only together with the proxy secret, and a sign-in without it fails. Any other sign-in of a directory account (`ldap_import`) looks the account up in LDAP and reads its `memberOf`. Local accounts, the bootstrap administrator and the MCP account are left to the reconcile. The settings are the `SNIPE_IT_LOGIN_RBAC_*` keys of [`templates/env.j2`](./templates/env.j2)."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role groups at sign-in:** ```files/Dockerfile`` <./files/Dockerfile>`__ installs the role-owned Composer package ```files/php/login_rbac/`` <./files/php/login_rbac/composer.json>`__ from a path repository, and Laravel’s package discovery loads its service provider, which registers ```SyncRolesOnLogin`` <./files/php/login_rbac/src/sync_roles_on_login.php>`__ for every ``Illuminate\\Auth\\Events\\Login``. It applies the rules of the RBAC reconcile to the account that signs in: ``superuser`` for the ``administrator`` role group, the ``web-app-snipe-it-user`` permission group for both role groups, neither for anyone else. An SSO sign-in takes its role groups from the ``X-Forwarded-Groups`` header of oauth2-proxy, which Apache copies into ``INFINITO_SSO_GROUPS`` only together with the proxy secret, and a sign-in without it fails. Any other sign-in of a directory account (``ldap_import``) looks the account up in LDAP and reads its ``memberOf``. Local accounts, the bootstrap administrator and the MCP account are left to the reconcile. The settings are the ``SNIPE_IT_LOGIN_RBAC_*`` keys of ```templates/env.j2`` <./templates/env.j2>`__."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role groups:** The oauth2-proxy gate admits only members of the ``administrator``, ``user`` and ``guest`` role groups, and the LDAP user filter finds only those members. The LDAP ``groups_to_roles`` plug-in maps the groups onto the Checkmk roles Administrator, Normal monitoring user and Guest user."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role groups:** The oauth2-proxy gate admits only members of the `administrator`, `user` and `guest` role groups, and the LDAP user filter finds only those members. The LDAP `groups_to_roles` plug-in maps the groups onto the Checkmk roles Administrator, Normal monitoring user and Guest user."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role groups:** The work splits by channel, because in swarm PeerTube and its database can run on different nodes and PeerTube publishes no host port there. At deploy time, once on the stack host, [tasks/rbac/provision.yml](./tasks/rbac/provision.yml) runs [files/python/rbac/provision.py](./files/python/rbac/provision.py) inside PeerTube's network namespace on its node: as root it creates the accounts of the `administrator` and `moderator` role group members that have an e-mail and no account yet. A member whose username or e-mail a local account of somebody else holds is not created but named as a warning, because that account could be a self-registration waiting for the member's role; the warning does not fail the deploy, so a stranger's signup cannot block it. A member whose username or e-mail an OpenID Connect account already holds is skipped, since PeerTube keeps the e-mail it first saw and the directory may have changed it since. It retries the whole run three times, 10 seconds apart; a 409 counts only when an account with the member's username and e-mail exists afterwards. A creation error that outlasts the retries fails the deploy after the hook below has run, so one bad member blocks neither the linking nor a demotion. The deploy also fails when the stack host is not among the play's hosts, instead of skipping the creation. Unlike the former hook, the timer does not retry a creation, so such a failure waits for the next deploy. [files/python/rbac/sync.py](./files/python/rbac/sync.py) then runs as a host hook of [sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync](../sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync/) on the node of the database container, at every deploy and on its timer, and only talks SQL: [files/sql/rbac/link-preprovisioned-accounts.sql](./files/sql/rbac/link-preprovisioned-accounts.sql) hands a local account to the OIDC plugin only when one role group member carries both its username and its e-mail and nobody has used it yet (no `lastLoginDate`, no OAuth token), so an account a failed deploy created but never linked is linked on the next run while an account someone registered and logged into is never taken over, and [files/sql/rbac/reconcile-roles.sql](./files/sql/rbac/reconcile-roles.sql) converges the PeerTube role of every OpenID Connect account to Administrator, Moderator or User from its groups. The same statement deletes every password-grant token of an OpenID Connect account: PeerTube refuses password logins to those, so such a token only stems from a login that read the account just before the link committed. A member added between deploys therefore has its role within one timer interval once the account exists, and gets a pre-provisioned account at the next deploy or its own at the first OIDC login. The local `root` account keeps its role, and its password never reaches the hook."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role groups:** The work splits by channel, because in swarm PeerTube and its database can run on different nodes and PeerTube publishes no host port there. At deploy time, once on the stack host, `tasks/rbac/provision.yml <./tasks/rbac/provision.yml>`__ runs `files/python/rbac/provision.py <./files/python/rbac/provision.py>`__ inside PeerTube’s network namespace on its node: as root it creates the accounts of the ``administrator`` and ``moderator`` role group members that have an e-mail and no account yet. A member whose username or e-mail a local account of somebody else holds is not created but named as a warning, because that account could be a self-registration waiting for the member’s role; the warning does not fail the deploy, so a stranger’s signup cannot block it. A member whose username or e-mail an OpenID Connect account already holds is skipped, since PeerTube keeps the e-mail it first saw and the directory may have changed it since. It retries the whole run three times, 10 seconds apart; a 409 counts only when an account with the member’s username and e-mail exists afterwards. A creation error that outlasts the retries fails the deploy after the hook below has run, so one bad member blocks neither the linking nor a demotion. The deploy also fails when the stack host is not among the play’s hosts, instead of skipping the creation. Unlike the former hook, the timer does not retry a creation, so such a failure waits for the next deploy. `files/python/rbac/sync.py <./files/python/rbac/sync.py>`__ then runs as a host hook of `sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync <../sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync/>`__ on the node of the database container, at every deploy and on its timer, and only talks SQL: `files/sql/rbac/link-preprovisioned-accounts.sql <./files/sql/rbac/link-preprovisioned-accounts.sql>`__ hands a local account to the OIDC plugin only when one role group member carries both its username and its e-mail and nobody has used it yet (no ``lastLoginDate``, no OAuth token), so an account a failed deploy created but never linked is linked on the next run while an account someone registered and logged into is never taken over, and `files/sql/rbac/reconcile-roles.sql <./files/sql/rbac/reconcile-roles.sql>`__ converges the PeerTube role of every OpenID Connect account to Administrator, Moderator or User from its groups. The same statement deletes every password-grant token of an OpenID Connect account: PeerTube refuses password logins to those, so such a token only stems from a login that read the account just before the link committed. A member added between deploys therefore has its role within one timer interval once the account exists, and gets a pre-provisioned account at the next deploy or its own at the first OIDC login. The local ``root`` account keeps its role, and its password never reaches the hook."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Role groups:** members of the ``administrator`` role group are superusers, members of ``reviewer`` join the built-in Reviewers team and members of ``manager`` the built-in Managers team; every other account loses the flag and both teams. `files/python/rbac_reconcile.py <./files/python/rbac_reconcile.py>`__ runs as an RBAC hook of `sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync <../sys-ctl-mtn-rbac-sync/>`__ at every deploy and on its timer; the platform administrator, bots and the anonymous account are never changed, and an account the OIDC sign-in creates is converged on the next run."
