msgid "A host with no previous environment file captures nothing and goes straight to the bootstrap path, which is the first deploy."
msgstr ""

msgid "A host without Mailu's maildir volume gets a note that there is nothing to import. Otherwise the deploy starts the import as the transient unit `stalwart-mailu-import` and moves on; a deploy that finds the unit still running leaves it alone. Follow it with `journalctl -fu stalwart-mailu-import`: it logs `[PROGRESS]` every 1000 messages of a folder, one `[MAILBOX] <mailbox> -> <account>: found=<n> imported=<n> skipped=<n> failed=<n> sieve=<state>` line per mailbox, one line per folder below it, `[SKIP]` for every mailbox outside the served domains, a closing `[SUMMARY]` and `[DONE] rc=<n>`. When the unit stops, it removes the staged credentials. An import that ends with `[DONE] rc=0` leaves the marker `.mailu-import.done` in the Stalwart instance directory, and no later deploy starts the import again while it exists; delete the marker to import again. A failed import leaves no marker, so the next deploy repeats it, and the repeat imports nothing twice: a message is skipped when its Message-ID, or without one its Date/From/To/Subject, is already in the target folder, and a Sieve script with unchanged content is left alone. Turn the switch off once Mailu is retired."
msgstr ""

msgid "A host without Mailu’s maildir volume gets a note that there is nothing to import. Otherwise the deploy starts the import as the transient unit ``stalwart-mailu-import`` and moves on; a deploy that finds the unit still running leaves it alone. Follow it with ``journalctl -fu stalwart-mailu-import``: it logs ``[PROGRESS]`` every 1000 messages of a folder, one ``[MAILBOX] <mailbox> -> <account>: found=<n> imported=<n> skipped=<n> failed=<n> sieve=<state>`` line per mailbox, one line per folder below it, ``[SKIP]`` for every mailbox outside the served domains, a closing ``[SUMMARY]`` and ``[DONE] rc=<n>``. When the unit stops, it removes the staged credentials. An import that ends with ``[DONE] rc=0`` leaves the marker ``.mailu-import.done`` in the Stalwart instance directory, and no later deploy starts the import again while it exists; delete the marker to import again. A failed import leaves no marker, so the next deploy repeats it, and the repeat imports nothing twice: a message is skipped when its Message-ID, or without one its Date/From/To/Subject, is already in the target folder, and a Sieve script with unchanged content is left alone. Turn the switch off once Mailu is retired."
msgstr ""

msgid "A host-mode-only provider such as svc-bkp-volume-2-local never gets a swarm row, so a swarm iteration on it deploys it inside another row's closure. Every row whose include set carries the provider qualifies, but those sets differ by more than an order of magnitude in what they pull. The rows are ranked by the summed ``min_storage`` of the include set the provision step derives, then by the complexity ``weight``, then by token, so the lightest host comes first."
msgstr ""

msgid "A host-mode-only role has no swarm row; deploy it inside the lightest host row \\`python -m cli.meta.ci.host\\_rows --provides <service key> --mode swarm\\` ranks first."
msgstr ""

msgid "A host-name entry covers the name and every name below it, so `example.org` and `*.example.org` are the same entry. dnsmasq matches on label boundaries and has no form for one host without its subdomains, so an exact-host meaning would promise something the resolver cannot keep. Only one leading `*.` is accepted. A CIDR with host bits set is refused, not widened, and a name whose last label is all digits is refused as a mistyped address."
msgstr ""

msgid "A host-name entry covers the name and every name below it. dnsmasq, which carries the decision onto the wire, matches on label boundaries the same way and has no form for one host without its subdomains, so ``example.org`` and ``*.example.org`` are the same entry. A narrower meaning here would promise an exactness the resolver cannot keep."
msgstr ""

msgid "A hosted runner that fills its disk, exhausts its RAM or wedges on a stuck mount dies without a trace: the Actions Worker stops, the deploy step stays ``in_progress`` and every diagnostic upload after it stays ``pending``, so no artifact survives. The watchdog samples the host while the step runs and ends the step itself once a floor is crossed, which turns a silent job loss into a failed step the post-mortem and upload steps still get to run after."
msgstr ""

msgid "A hub named in the inventory for Compose deployments, with roaming spokes that dial it with `PersistentKeepalive` and are never waited for."
msgstr ""

msgid "A hub named in the inventory for Compose deployments, with roaming spokes that dial it with ``PersistentKeepalive`` and are never waited for."
